# Chapter 5, part 5: the markers from Revision to Whitespace, and the markers
# that are printed as a label.

.h2 Revision [marker-revision]
.ix Revision marker
.ix Marker, Revision
.ix Revision number in text
A Revision marker prints the revision number of the document, the number that
rises by one thousandth every time a changed document is saved.  Use it on the
title page of a draft that goes round to several people, so that the paper says
which version it is.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | None.  The marker is inserted at once.
Source of the | The Revision field of the Save panel, kept in the header of
 text | the document ({n:file-revision}).
.end
.h3 On the screen
Long and Short, the default being Long: the revision with three decimals in
brackets, such as [1.007].  A new document is 1.000.  Name: [REV].
.h3 When printed
The revision as text, with three decimals (1.007), by the display mode of the
marker ({n:markers-print-modes}).  The number is the one that the document has
when the print is made, which is the number of the last Save: a document that
was changed after the last Save prints the old number, because the number
rises when the document is saved ({n:file-revision-rule}).
.h3 Edges
- The number is not a copy: when it is changed on the Save panel, all the
  Revision markers of the document print the new number at the next print.
- The number is always written with three decimals: 2 is 2.000.
- A document that has no revision number is revision 1.000.

.h2 Section [marker-section]
.ix Section marker
.ix Marker, Section
.ix Section in a header or footer
A Section marker prints the number, the title or both of the section that a
page belongs to.  It is made for headers and footers: {c:\section\},
{c:\sectionnumber\} and {c:\sectiontitle\} in the Header or Footer field of
a page layout are this marker ({n:format-page-headers}).
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | Show.
Place | A header or a footer, as for the Page Number marker
 ({n:marker-page-number}).
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
Show | Number and Title, Number Only, Title Only | Number and Title | What the
 marker prints.
.end
The panel of a Section marker that stands in the text is opened with
{k:Ctrl-F12} on it ({n:format-page-headers}).
.h3 On the screen
Name, the default: [SECTION].  Short and Long show [SECTION] as well.
.h3 When printed
The section whose headline is the first one on the page, or the section of
the page before when the page has no headline.  The number is the tag of the
headline as it is printed.
.h3 Edges
- A headline that has no tag gives its title for every choice.
- The pages before the first headline show nothing.

.h2 Time [marker-time]
.ix Time marker
.ix Marker, Time
.ix Time format
.ix H, HH, MM, SS, am/pm
A Time marker prints the time of day at which the document is printed.  Use it
with the Date marker ({n:marker-date}) on a page that must show when the paper
was made.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | Format.
Global setting | Time Format on the Marker Format panel of Global, Marker
  | ({n:format-marker-datetime}).
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
Format | Text made of the | H:MM am/pm | How the time is written.
  | time words below | (or the
  | and ordinary | Time
  | characters | Format of
  |  | the
  |  | document)
.end
The words are not case sensitive.  Anything else in the format is printed as it
is.
.table 6 54
Word | Meaning
H | The hour of the twelve-hour clock, without a zero in front: 6.
HH | The hour of the twelve-hour clock as two digits: 06.
MM | The minutes as two digits: 20.
SS | The seconds as two digits: 39.
am/pm | The letters am or pm: am before noon, pm from noon.  The words are
  | written together with the slash.
.end
.table 20 40
Format | Gives (twenty past six in the morning and 39
  | seconds)
H:MM am/pm | 6:20 am
HH:MM:SS | 06:20:39
H:MM | 6:20
HH:MM am/pm | 06:20 am
.end
The hours are always those of a twelve-hour clock: there is no word for a
twenty-four-hour clock, and 15:05 is 3:05 pm.  A single M is not a word in a
time (the minutes are MM); the letters am without the slash and pm are not the
word am/pm and print
as they are.
.caution The words H, HH, MM, SS and am/pm are found wherever they stand in the
  format, so a text of your own in the format may be changed: in the format
  "Time: H:MM" the letters of Time are not words, but "Hours: H" prints the h
  of Hours as an hour.  Words in a format must not hold those letters.
.h3 On the screen
Long and Short: the time of now in brackets, such as [6:20 am].  The time is the
local time of the system.  Name: [TIME].
.h3 When printed
The time at the moment of the print, as text, by the display mode of the marker
({n:markers-print-modes}).  Final and Draft are the same.
.h3 Edges
- A format that is left as the default follows the Time Format of the Global
  menu.
- An empty field is the default: a format cannot be made of nothing.
- The Filetime marker ({n:marker-filetime}) uses the same format words for the
  time of the file.

.h2 Whitespace [marker-whitespace]
.ix Whitespace marker
.ix Marker, Whitespace
.ix Vertical space
.ix Blank space for artwork
.ix Treatment at end of page
A Whitespace marker leaves a vertical space of a given height in the text.  Use
it to make room for a picture that is pasted in by hand, for a signature, or for
a gap that a blank line is too small for.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | Size (vertical), Unit, Treatment at End of Page.
Reached | The Marker Commands table, entry Whitespace.
Place | Best in a block of its own.
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
Size (vertical) | A number with up to three decimals | None (required) | The
 height of the space, in the Unit.  An empty field gives the message No string
 specified.
Unit | in, cm, mm, pt, bp, pc, dd, cc, sp, em, ex | in | The unit of the size
 ({n:markers-panels}).
Treatment at End of Page | Crop at End of Page, Place All on Next Page, Float
 to Next Page | Place All on Next Page | What happens on paper when the space
 does not fit on what is left of the page (see below).  It is also shown in the
 label of the marker.
.end
.pic pictures/markers-whitespace.png 5.4 The panel of the Whitespace marker: a space of 0.5 centimeter that is placed on the next page when it does not fit.
.h3 On the screen
The default mode is Short.  Short: [WS 3 in] with the size and the unit, written
without trailing zeros (1.5 cm).  Long: [WHITESPACE 3 in, Place All on Next
Page] with the name of the treatment.  Name: [WS].  The screen does not leave
the space.
.h3 When printed
Final and Draft:
- The paragraph in which the marker stands ends there, and a space of the size
  and the unit is left; the text after the marker begins after the space.  The
  space is kept at the top of a page.
- A Size of 0 prints nothing, not even the end of the paragraph.
- In the cell of a table, or in a kept region, the space is left as a height
  in the line.
- A space that fits on what is left of the page is left in full, whatever the
  Treatment says.
- When the space does not fit, the Treatment says what is done.  Crop at End of
  Page ends the page where the space begins; the space is cut off at the end
  of the page and none of it is carried over, so the text after the marker
  begins at the top of the next page.  Place All on Next Page ends the page
  where the space begins and leaves the whole space at the top of the next
  page; the text after the marker follows it.  Float to Next Page leaves the
  whole space at the top of the next page too, but the text after the marker
  moves up in front of it and fills the rest of the page.
.h3 Edges
- A size taller than the text area of a page (9 inches on Letter paper with
  margins of 1 inch) is a whole page of space: the space is cut to the height
  of the text area, and the text after the marker follows on the next page.
- The Draft print is set in single spacing, but a Whitespace marker is a length
  of its own: a space of 1 inch is 1 inch in Draft as well.
.h3 Messages
No string specified; Not a valid number (Size (vertical)).

.h2 Markers that are printed as a Box [markers-labels]
.ix Markers printed as a label
.ix Box
.ix Figure marker
.ix Table marker
.ix Index marker
.ix Merge marker
Four markers hold a name for the document and are printed as a Box: the label
of the marker in a small typewriter face in a thin frame, in the place where
the marker stands.  They are Figure, Index, Merge and Table.  A Box
takes the place of the marker and changes nothing else on the page, so the text
around it is printed whole.  (The markers If, Else and Endif are not printed at
all; they select text, see {n:marker-if}.  The Include marker prints the text
of another document, see {n:marker-include}.)
Their panels are filled in as for any marker ({n:markers-panels}), and the
label that each prints is the one that the Long mode shows
({n:markers-screen}).  Their default display modes are in the table of
{x:markers-table}.
.table 10 15 32
Marker | Panel | Label printed
Figure | Name (for Table of Figures), may be empty | FIGURE "name"
Index | Main Heading (required), Subheading, Reference Text | INDEX followed by
 | | the main heading
Merge | Variable Name (required) | MERGE "name", Delete:Yes
Table | Name (for Table of Tables) (required) | Table
.end
An empty required field gives the message No string specified, and the name of
a file or a field is not looked up when the marker is made.
.h3 Figure and Table [marker-figure]
.ix List of figures
.ix List of tables
A Figure marker and a Table marker give a name to a figure or a table.  Put
one just before or after the picture, equation or table that it names.  The
Picture marker has the same field ({n:marker-picture}), so a picture does not
need a Figure marker next to it; its name makes an entry of the list of
pictures, not of the list of figures.  The Figure is shown as [FIG "name"] by default
(its Short mode) and prints FIGURE with the name between double quotation
marks; the Table shows as [TABLE "name"] in Short and Long mode and prints a
Box that holds only the word Table.  When the print makes the list of figures or of tables
({n:print-index}) the marker prints nothing at its place.  The first marker of
the kind in a document turns its list on, and the last one taken out turns it
off again, unless the document has made the choice itself (Global, Format,
fourth panel).  The name of a Table
may not be empty.  A table of Strata is a
block of several columns ({n:view-block}); it needs no Table marker.
.h3 Index [marker-index]
.ix Index entries
An Index marker holds one entry for an index of the document: a Main Heading,
a Subheading and a Reference Text that replaces the page number, such as See
Map.  Put it at the word or the paragraph that the entry is for.  A subject
that has several headings needs one marker for each heading.  The default mode
is Short; Short and Long show INDEX followed by the main heading, Diamond
shows a diamond, and the Box prints the same.  When the print makes the index ({n:print-index}) the
marker prints nothing and the index lists its page.
.pic pictures/markers-index.png 5.4 The panel of the Index marker, with the main heading Strata and the subheading Markers.
.h3 Merge [marker-merge]
.ix Variable name
A Merge marker holds a Variable Name.  It prints as the Box MERGE "NAME",
Delete:Yes.
.h2 Include: a work made of parts [marker-include]
.ix Include marker
.ix Master document
.ix Parts of a document
.ix Several authors
.ix Collaborative editing
An Include marker puts the text of another document at its place in the print.
It is made for a work that several people write at the same time.  Only one
person can edit a document at a time, so each author keeps a part of the work
in a document of their own, and a {b:master document} holds an Include marker
for every part.  One print or one preview of the master is the whole work,
made of the text that each part has when the print is made.  A single long
document needs no parts: a document of thousands of pages is edited, searched
and printed as one, by one person at a time.  Use Include to share the work,
not to cut a long document into pieces.  Working with several authors, below,
tells how to set it up ({n:authors}).
.h3 The panel
The Include marker is on the Marker Commands menu and has one field.
.table 10 50
Field | What to put in it
File Name | The name of the part.  It is required.  It may be written with or
 without the extension {c:.doc}, with capital or small letters, and with a
 directory in front of it, such as {c:chapters/two}.  A name that begins with
 a slash is a full path.  {k:Keypad +}, {k:Alt-+} or {k:F2} fills the field
 from a checklist of the documents ({n:file-checklist}).
.end
.pic pictures/markers-include.png 5.4 The panel of the Include marker.
On the screen the marker shows as INCLUDE "name" in the Long mode, like any
marker ({n:markers-screen}).  The screen of the master shows the master only:
the text of a part is not drawn there, and a section number on the screen of
the master is the number that the section has in the master.  To read or
change a part, open its document.  The name is not looked up when the marker
is made; it is looked up when the work is printed.
.h3 Where the marker goes
Put the marker alone in a block: nothing else in that block but spaces.  The
block is taken out of the print and the part is put in its place.
.table 20 40
The part | What is put at the marker
Has no sections | Its blocks, one after the other, in the section that holds
 the marker.
Has sections | Its sections, one level below the section that holds the
 marker, in front of the sections that this section already has.  The text
 of its title page, if any, comes first, in the section of the marker.  The
 marker must be in the last block of its section, and not in a headline.
.end
In a document that has no sections the blocks of a part are put at the marker
and the headlines of the part are printed as blocks of text.
.h3 What is printed [marker-include-print]
.ix Include marker, print
.ix Master document, print
Final, Draft, Preview and Export a LaTeX File all put the parts in, and so does
a print of the current section or of a range of sections when the marker is
inside it.  The rest of this section tells what the print makes of the work.
.table 20 40
Thing | What is done
Text of a part | Read from its file when the print is made.  The print has
 the text that was last saved.  A change that is open in the Editor and not
 saved is not in the print: save the part first.
Numbers | The sections of the parts are numbered as the sections of one
 document: the tags, the counters, the notes, the cross-references and the
 pages go on through the parts.  The contents lists the headlines of the
 parts with their pages.
Cross-references | A cross-reference points at a section of the document
 that holds it, and in the print it gives the number that the section has in
 the whole work.
Page layout and formats | The Global Format of the master is used: the page,
 the margins, the headers and the footers, the fonts, the tags and the
 formats of the notes and of the counters.  A part brings its text, its
 pictures, its markers and the formats of its blocks and of its sections.  The
 Global Format of a part is not used; a part that has a named format that the
 master has not is printed with the formats that the master gives.
Conditional text | The variables of the print select the text of the parts
 as they select the text of the master ({n:marker-if-print}).
Log of the run | Lists every file that was read: its path, its size and the
 time of its last change.
.end
.h3 Where a part is looked for
.ix Include marker, file names
A name that begins with a slash is taken as it is.  Any other name is looked for
in the directory of the document that holds the marker (the master, or the part
that includes another part), and then in the working directory.  The extension
{c:.doc} may be left out, and the case of the letters does not matter.  Keep
the master and its parts in one directory, and the work can be copied to
another computer and printed there.
.h3 Parts inside parts
A part may hold Include markers of its own, up to eight levels deep.  A part
that includes itself, or that includes a part that includes it again, is a
loop: the second include is not made and a mistake is told.
.h3 Mistakes in an Include [marker-include-errors]
.ix Include marker, errors
.ix Invalid Include request
.ix Error Log, Include
These are mistakes of the document: a marker with no file name, a file that
cannot be found or read, a file that is not a document or is damaged, a loop,
more than eight levels, a marker with other text in its block, a marker in a
headline, and a part with sections at a marker that is not in the last block
of its section.  The print and the Preview are made all the same, and each
mistake is told in two ways.  The Error Log lists it as
{c:Invalid Include request} with the reason, at the place of the marker of the
master (for a mistake inside a part, the marker of the master that brought the
part in) ({n:print-errors}).  And a notice, in square brackets, is printed at
that place: {c:[Invalid Include request: The file NAME cannot be found.]}.  A
marker that has other text in its block is left as it is and is printed as a
Box.  The messages and what to do about them are in {x:msg-errlog-faults}.
.h2 Working with several authors [authors]
.ix Several authors
.ix Collaborative editing
.ix Master document
A group writes one work at the same time in these steps.
.table 5 55
Step | What to do
1 | Agree on the parts, one for each author: a chapter, a section, a part of
 the data.  Make a directory for the work and a document for each part.
2 | Make the master document in the same directory.  Give it the Global Format
 of the work: the page, the margins, the headers and the footers, the fonts
 and the tags.  Give it the title page and the headlines that the authors
 share, and for each part an Include marker alone in a block, in the last
 block of the section that is to hold the part.
3 | Each author opens the document of their own part and writes in it as in
 any document.  The sections of a part are numbered by their place in the
 whole work, not in the part, so there is no need to number them by hand.
 To see a part on its own, print it: it is printed with its own Global
 Format.
4 | Each author saves the part often.  The print reads the files, so it has
 what each author has saved and nothing more.
5 | Print or Preview the master whenever the whole work is wanted: the text of
 every part as it is saved, in the order of the markers, with the numbers, the
 notes, the cross-references, the contents and the pages of one document.  The
 master has the contents of the whole work when its Table of Contents Level
 is not 0 ({n:format-fields-lists}), and its lists and its index have the
 markers of every part.
.end
These are the limits.
- Strata does not lock a file.  Nothing prevents two people from opening the
  same part and changing it; the one who saves last replaces the other's
  changes.  Agree on who writes which part.
- The screen shows the master only.  The whole work is seen in a Preview or in
  a print.
- The master prints the parts that its markers name, and no others.  A
  part that is not named is not in the work.
- The cross-references of a document point at the sections of that document
  only; to point from one part to another, name the section in words.
- The pictures of a part are looked for where the pictures of the master are
  looked for, so keep the pictures with the master.
.h2 Conditional text: If, Else and Endif [marker-if]
.ix Conditional text
.ix If marker
.ix Else marker
.ix Endif marker
.ix Expression of the If marker
.ix Variables, in the If marker
Conditional text is text that a print has for one use of a document and not
for another: a paragraph for one region, a note for the staff, a table for
the long version.  Three markers make it.  The {b:If} marker holds a condition.
The text after it, up to the {b:Else} marker (or up to the {b:Endif} marker when
there is no Else), is printed when the condition is true.  The text between
Else and Endif is printed when it is false.  Endif ends the condition.  The
If marker is on the Marker Commands menu with a field for its condition; Else
and Endif have no field.
.h3 What is printed [marker-if-print]
.ix If marker, print
.ix Conditional text, print and preview
Print, Preview and Export a LaTeX File choose a branch by the condition and
leave the other out of the pages together with the three markers: nothing of
them is printed, not even a Box.  The text of a branch can be a few words in a
line, whole blocks, a table or a picture.  A condition may be inside the
branch of another, up to ten deep.  The screen, Search and the other export (the
text file) show all of the text with the markers where they stand, so that the
whole document can be worked on in one place.
A section that stands inside the text of a branch that is left out is left out
with its headline.  The sections that follow are numbered again as if it had
never been there, and the cross-references to them and the contents follow;
a cross-reference to a section that is left out says that its section is not
there; see {x:print-errors}.  The Index markers and the pictures of the text
that is left out are not used.
.h3 The condition [marker-if-expression]
.ix If marker, expression
.ix Expression, operators
The one field of the If marker, Expression, holds three parts: the name of a
variable, an operator and a value.  Spaces around the parts do not matter.  The value is
the rest of the line, so it may hold spaces inside it.
.table 10 50
Operator | The condition is true when
= | the variable is equal to the value.
{c:#} | the variable is not equal to the value.
< | the variable is less than the value.
> | the variable is greater than the value.
<= | the variable is less than or equal to the value.
>= | the variable is greater than or equal to the value.
.end
A not equal is written with the number sign: {c:<>} and {c:!=} are not
operators.  A value is made of printable characters of 7-bit ASCII, at most 255
of them, and it has no quotation marks (neither the double nor the single
one), so it needs no quoting: {c:region = North America} compares the variable
with the text North America.  There is nothing but one comparison in a
condition: no and, no or, no arithmetic.
.table 29 31
Field of the If marker | Meaning
{c:COLOR = red} | True when COLOR is red.
{c:region # EU} | True when region is anything but EU.
{c:version >= 12} | True when version is 12 or more.
{c:.STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release} | True in anything but a release.
{c:edition = undefined} | True when no edition was given.
.end
.h3 Variables [marker-variables]
.ix Variables
.ix Variable names
.ix Undefined variable
.ix Variables, Edit / Preview / Print Document
The variables are given when a document is edited, previewed or printed
({n:str-variables}).  A name starts with a letter and holds letters, digits,
hyphens and underscores, such as {c:region}, {c:Print-Run} or {c:v2_draft}.
A value is any text of printable 7-bit ASCII, up to 255 characters, with no
quotation marks.  No more than eight variables are given at a time.  A value
is the text that was typed: a variable has no calculation in it and cannot
refer to another variable.  Whoever needs a value that is worked out makes
the value simple before it is given: a region number is {c:09}, a country
code {c:SE}.
A variable that was not given, and one whose value is empty, has the value
{c:undefined}.  That is also how to test for it: {c:region = undefined} is
true when no region was given.
.h3 The special variables [marker-special-variables]
.ix Special variables
.ix .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE
.ix Build type
A name that begins with a period belongs to Strata.  The special variables
are given by Strata itself and cannot be given by the user; the panels refuse
a name with a period.  They can be used in the condition of an If marker like
any other.
.table 20 40
Variable | Value
.STRATA_BUILD_TYPE | {c:release} in the version that is installed for users,
 | {c:debug} or {c:asan} in the versions that the developers of Strata make for
 | their work.
.end
.h3 Comparing [marker-compare]
.ix Comparing values
.ix Numbers and text, comparing
.ix Order of text, ASCII
A comparison is a comparison of numbers when both sides are numbers, and a
comparison of text in every other case.  A side is a number when it is made
only of the digits 0 to 9 and is not greater than 4294967295: no sign, no
decimal point, no space.  Leading zeros do not count, so {c:09} is the number 9.
Numbers are compared by their value: {c:9} is less than {c:10}.
All other values, and a pair in which one side is a number and the other is
not, are compared as text, character by character, in the order of the ASCII
codes: the digits come before the capital letters and the capital letters
before the small ones, a capital letter is not the same as the small letter,
and a text is less than a longer text that begins with it.
In the table the variable is v, and the value that it was given is written
after the comparison.
.table 28 8 21
Comparison | True? | Why
{c:v < 10}, v is 9 | Yes | Both are numbers.
{c:v = 9}, v is 09 | Yes | Both are numbers: 9 and 9.
{c:v > 4294967294}, v is 4294967295 | Yes | Both are numbers.
{c:v > 9}, v is 4294967296 | No | The first is too large to be a number.
{c:v < a}, v is Z | Yes | Text: the capital letters come first.
{c:v = Red}, v is red | No | Text: the letters are not the same.
{c:v < abd}, v is abc | Yes | Text: c comes before d.
{c:v > 10}, v is not given | Yes | Text: u comes after 1.
.end
The last row matters: a variable that was not given is the text
{c:undefined}, so {c:version > 10} is true when no version was given.  When
that is not wanted, ask for a missing variable first: put the condition
{c:version = undefined} in front of it.  No comparison fails a print: it is
only true or false.
.h3 Mistakes in a condition [marker-if-errors]
.ix If marker, errors
.ix Conditional text, errors
.ix Error Log, conditions
A condition that cannot be read, an Else or an Endif that has no If before it,
an If that has no Endif after it and more than ten conditions inside one
another are mistakes of the document.  The print is made, and the Error Log
lists each of them with its place ({n:print-errors}).  The branch of a
condition that cannot be read is left out as if it were false.  An If that is
never closed holds the rest of the document.  The messages and what to do
about them are in {x:msg-errlog-faults}.
